# University Physics Volume 3, chapters 9-11 (condensed matter, nuclei, particles and cosmology)

§ 3 9.0
P Chapter 8 built the electron structure of a single atom from the [[tise|Schrödinger equation]] and the [[pauli|exclusion principle]]. This chapter brings atoms together. Two atoms can lower their energy by transferring or sharing electrons, and the molecules they form rotate and vibrate with quantized energies. In a solid, about 10²³ atoms per cubic centimetre bind into a regular array, and their outer electrons occupy bands of allowed energies. How these bands are filled explains why metals conduct and insulators do not, how small amounts of impurity control a semiconductor, and why some metals lose all electrical resistance at low temperature.
§ 3 9.1
P A [[bonds|molecular bond]] holds atoms together when their total energy is lower together than apart. Bonds are of three main kinds: ionic, covalent and van der Waals. In an *ionic bond* one atom gives an electron to the other. The [[econf|electron configurations]] of sodium and chlorine show why this can pay. Removing sodium's single 3s electron costs its ionization energy, 5.14 eV, while chlorine, one electron short of a filled 3p shell, releases 3.61 eV, its *electron affinity*, when it gains one. The transfer therefore costs E_{transfer} = 1.53 eV. The two ions then attract by [[coul|Coulomb's law]]. At short range their filled shells overlap, and the [[pauli|exclusion principle]] pushes electrons into higher states, which adds a steep repulsion U_{ex} = A/rⁿ. The total energy is lowest at the *equilibrium separation distance* r₀, or *bond length*, and the depth of this minimum is the *dissociation energy*.
Q U_{form} = E_{transfer} + U_{coul} + U_{ex},   U_{coul}(r₀) = −ke²/r₀
N M bonds | Molecular bonds | ionic, covalent, van der Waals | q
d Atoms bind by transferring an electron (ionic bond, energy set by ionization energy, electron affinity and Coulomb attraction), by sharing a pair of electrons in an antisymmetric spin state (covalent bond, exchange symmetry, hybridization), or by induced dipoles (van der Waals bond).
p equilibrium separation where U(r) is minimum; dissociation energy = depth of the minimum; covalent bonding needs the exclusion principle; polyatomic molecules from hybrid orbitals
g ionic bond; covalent bond; van der Waals bond; electron affinity; equilibrium separation distance; dissociation energy; exchange symmetry; polyatomic molecule; hybridization
R[thm] bonds <- coul, econf, pauli : U = E_transfer + U_coul + U_ex | Energy of formation | Coulomb attraction balanced by exclusion-principle repulsion.
P For a sodium chloride molecule r₀ = 0.236 nm, so U_{coul} = −(1.44 eV·nm)/(0.236 nm) = −6.10 eV and E_{transfer} + U_{coul} = −4.57 eV. The measured dissociation energy is about 4.3 eV; the remaining 0.3 eV is the repulsion U_{ex} at r₀. In a *covalent bond* two atoms share a pair of electrons instead. The wave function of two electrons must change sign when they are exchanged, a requirement called *exchange symmetry*. If the spin part is antisymmetric, with the two spins opposite, the spatial part is symmetric and piles charge up between the nuclei, where it attracts both. This is how two hydrogen atoms bind into H₂, with r₀ = 0.074 nm and a dissociation energy of about 4.5 eV. Parallel spins would force an antisymmetric spatial part with little charge between the nuclei, and no bond forms.
P In a *polyatomic molecule* the bonds point in definite directions. In methane, CH₄, the 2s orbital and the three 2p orbitals of carbon combine into four equivalent orbitals aimed at the corners of a tetrahedron, 109.5° apart; this mixing is called *hybridization*. Atoms with filled shells still attract one another weakly. A momentary dipole in one atom induces a dipole in its neighbour, and the two dipoles attract. This *van der Waals bond* has energies of order 0.01 eV, enough to hold noble gases together as solids at low temperature.

§ 3 9.2
P A diatomic molecule can rotate about its centre of mass and vibrate along its bond; the quantized motions give the [[molspec|molecular energy levels]]. As a rigid rotor it has the [[I|moment of inertia]] I = μr₀², where μ = m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂) is the reduced mass. Its [[Lq|angular momentum]] is quantized, L² = l(l + 1)ħ², so the *rotational energy levels* are E_r = l(l + 1)E_{0r}, with E_{0r} = ħ²/(2I). Near r₀ the [[bonds|bond]] energy is parabolic, so the vibration is a [[qho|quantum harmonic oscillator]] with *vibrational energy levels* (n + ½)ħω. *Electric dipole transitions*, the main way light is absorbed, obey the *selection rules* Δn = ±1 and Δl = ±1. The infrared spectrum is therefore a band of lines spaced by 2E_{0r} on both sides of ħω, with no line at ħω itself. For HCl, μ ≈ 0.98 u and r₀ = 0.127 nm give E_{0r} ≈ 1.3 meV, against ħω ≈ 0.36 eV. The spacing thus measures I and r₀.
Q E_r = l(l + 1)ħ²/(2I),   E_v = (n + ½)ħω
N S molspec | Molecular energy levels | E_r = l(l+1)ħ²/2I,  E_v = (n + ½)ħω | q
d A diatomic molecule rotates and vibrates with quantized energies; electric dipole transitions obey Δl = ±1 and Δn = ±1, giving band spectra.
m rotational levels E_r = l(l+1)ħ²/(2I), I = μr₀²; vibrational levels (n + ½)ħω
g rotational energy level; electric dipole transition; selection rule; vibrational energy level
R[thm] molspec <- qho, Lq, I, bonds : rigid rotor plus harmonic bond | Rotor and oscillator | Quantized rotation and vibration of the bonded pair.
P The same levels explain why the heat capacity of a gas departs from classical [[equip|equipartition]]. A mode takes up energy only if k_{B}T, with k_{B} the [[kB|Boltzmann constant]], is comparable with its level spacing. When the spacing is much larger, nearly all molecules stay in the lowest level and the mode is frozen. For H₂ the [[molspec|molecular energy levels]] have E_{0r} ≈ 7.5 meV and ħω ≈ 0.55 eV, while k_{B}T ≈ 0.026 eV at 300 K. Rotation is active and vibration is frozen, so the [[CV|molar heat capacity at constant volume]] is close to (5/2)R rather than (7/2)R. Rotation freezes in turn as the temperature falls below about 100 K, and near 50 K C_V is close to (3/2)R, the value for translation alone.
R[thm] CV <- molspec, kB : modes with level spacing ≫ k_BT do not absorb heat | Frozen degrees of freedom explained | Why hydrogen's C_V falls at low temperature.

§ 3 9.3
P In a crystalline solid, atoms or ions sit on a [[lattice|crystal lattice]], a pattern repeated in three dimensions. Three cubic patterns are common: *simple cubic*, with an atom at each corner of a cube; *body-centred cubic* (BCC), with one more at the centre of the cube; and *face-centred cubic* (FCC), with one more at the centre of each face. The pattern and its spacing d are measured by X-ray diffraction through [[bragg|Bragg's law]], 2d sin θ = mλ. In an ionic crystal each ion feels the [[bonds|ionic bonding]] of all the others. The Coulomb energy per ion pair is −αke²/r, where the *Madelung constant* α sums the attractions and repulsions over the whole lattice; α = 1.748 for sodium chloride. Adding a repulsion A/rⁿ, whose exponent n is called the *repulsion constant*, and setting dU/dr = 0 at r = r₀ gives the energy needed to separate the crystal into ions, per ion pair.
Q U_{diss} = α(ke²/r₀)(1 − 1/n)
N S lattice | Crystal lattice | simple cubic, BCC, FCC | q
d Atoms or ions in a solid arranged in a repeating lattice (simple cubic, body-centered cubic, face-centered cubic); the Madelung constant sums the Coulomb energy of an ionic crystal, and a repulsion constant sets the short-range repulsion.
m periodic arrangement (Bravais lattice with a basis) with translational symmetry
g lattice; face-centered cubic (FCC); simple cubic; body-centered cubic (BCC); repulsion constant; Madelung constant
R[thm] lattice <- bonds, bragg : U_diss = α ke²/r₀ (1 − 1/n) | Bonding in a crystal | Structure measured by X-ray diffraction.
P For sodium chloride, r₀ = 0.282 nm, so αke²/r₀ = 1.748 × (1.44 eV·nm)/(0.282 nm) = 8.93 eV. With n ≈ 9 the dissociation energy is 8.93 eV × 8/9 ≈ 7.9 eV per ion pair, within a few percent of the measured value. The repulsion lowers the result by only the fraction 1/n because it rises so steeply. Each ion has six nearest neighbours of opposite charge, and α > 1 expresses the extra binding this gives compared with a single ion pair. In metals the outer electrons leave their atoms and are shared by the whole lattice; this *metallic bonding* holds together iron, which is BCC at room temperature, and copper, which is FCC. Glass and many plastics lack long-range order. Such *amorphous solids* have no lattice, although each atom still has a well-defined set of near neighbours.

§ 3 9.4
P In the *free electron model* of a metal, the valence electrons move freely through the [[cond|conductor]] and are held in only at its surfaces. Classically they form an ideal gas; their *electron number density* N/V is 8.5 × 10²⁸ m⁻³ in copper, one per atom. Quantum mechanically the metal is a cubic [[pib|particle in a box]] of side L, with energies E = (π²ħ²/(2mₑL²))(n₁² + n₂² + n₃²) for positive integers n₁, n₂, n₃. The [[pauli|exclusion principle]] allows two electrons, with opposite spins, in each spatial state. At T = 0 the electrons therefore fill the lowest states up to the *Fermi energy* E_F. Counting the states inside one octant of a sphere in the space of (n₁, n₂, n₃) gives E_F in terms of the density alone. This filled set of states is the [[fermigas|free electron gas]].
Q E_F = (h²/8mₑ)(3N/πV)^{2/3}
N S fermigas | Free electron gas | E_F = (h²/8m)(3N/πV)^{2/3} | q
d Conduction electrons as particles in a three-dimensional box, filling the lowest states two per level (exclusion principle) up to the Fermi energy; occupation at temperature T follows the Fermi factor.
m filled set of box states up to E_F; density of states g(E) ∝ E^{1/2}; Fermi temperature T_F = E_F/k_B
g free electron model; Fermi energy; electron number density; density of states; Fermi factor; Fermi temperature
R[thm] fermigas <- pib, pauli, cond : E = (π²ħ²/2mL²)(n₁² + n₂² + n₃²), two electrons per state | Box states filled | The classical free-electron gas made quantum.
P For copper the formula gives E_F = 7.0 eV. The *Fermi temperature* T_F = E_F/k_{B} is then 8.2 × 10⁴ K, while k_{B}T is only 0.026 eV at 300 K. Only electrons within a few k_{B}T of E_F can move to empty states when the metal is heated, which is why conduction electrons add almost nothing to its heat capacity, contrary to the classical gas model. The number of states per unit energy, the *density of states*, grows as g(E) ∝ E^{1/2}. At temperature T a state of energy E is occupied with the probability given by the *Fermi factor*, f(E) = 1/[exp((E − E_F)/k_{B}T) + 1]. It equals ½ at E_F and drops from nearly 1 to nearly 0 over a few k_{B}T around it.

§ 3 9.5
P The free electron model ignores the ions. In a crystal the electrons move in the periodic potential of the [[lattice|lattice]], and two approaches give their allowed energies. One starts from isolated atoms. When two atoms form a [[bonds|bond]], each atomic level splits into two; when N atoms are brought together and their wave functions overlap, each level splits into N closely spaced levels. With N near 10²³ these form an *energy band*. The other approach starts from the [[fermigas|free electron gas]] and adds a weak periodic potential. Electron waves whose wavelength satisfies the Bragg condition for the lattice are reflected, and standing waves at those wavelengths take two different energies. Both approaches give [[bands|energy bands]] separated by *energy gaps*, intervals of energy with no allowed states.
N S bands | Energy bands | bands and gaps | q
d In a crystal the atomic levels broaden into energy bands separated by energy gaps; a filled valence band with a large gap gives an insulator, a small gap a semiconductor, a partly filled band a conductor.
m allowed energy intervals (valence band, conduction band) separated by forbidden gaps
g energy band; energy gap; valence band; conduction band; semiconductor
R[thm] bands <- fermigas, lattice, bonds : N atoms ⇒ each level splits into N closely spaced levels | Atomic levels broadened | Periodic potential opens gaps.
P Conduction depends on how the bands are filled. In sodium the band formed from the 3s levels can hold 2N electrons but receives only N, so it is half full. A small electric field can move electrons into nearby empty states of the same band at almost no energy cost, so the metal is a [[cond|conductor]]. In an insulator or a semiconductor at T = 0, the highest filled band, the *valence band*, is full, and the empty band above it is the *conduction band*. A full band carries no current. When the gap is wide, 5.5 eV in diamond, thermal energy k_{B}T ≈ 0.026 eV at 300 K almost never lifts an electron across it, and the crystal is an [[insul|insulator]]. A *semiconductor* has a narrower gap, 1.1 eV in silicon, so a small number of electrons are thermally excited into the conduction band. Its conductivity rises with temperature, unlike that of a metal. Both behaviours follow from the [[bands|band]] structure.
R[thm] cond <- bands : partly filled band ⇒ free carriers | Conductors explained | Electrons can move into empty states at almost no energy cost.
R[thm] insul <- bands : full band and wide gap ⇒ no free carriers | Insulators explained | No empty states nearby.

§ 3 9.6
P An electron excited into the conduction band of a semiconductor leaves an empty state in the valence band, a *hole*. A neighbouring electron can move into the hole, so the hole moves through the crystal like a positive charge +e. Adding a small fraction of *impurity atoms*, called *doping*, controls which carrier dominates. Silicon uses all four valence electrons of its [[econf|electron configuration]] in bonds to its neighbours. A phosphorus atom, from group V, brings a fifth electron, bound by only about 0.05 eV in a *donor impurity* level just below the conduction band. At room temperature most of these electrons are freed, and the material is an *n-type semiconductor*. A boron atom, from group III, brings only three; its empty *acceptor impurity* level just above the valence band captures an electron from that band and leaves a hole, giving a *p-type semiconductor*. At high doping these levels broaden into an *impurity band*. A [[doping|doped semiconductor]] is thus the [[bands|band structure]] plus impurity levels in the gap.
N S doping | Doped semiconductor | n-type, p-type | q
d Impurity atoms add donor levels (n-type, extra electrons) or acceptor levels (p-type, holes) in the gap; the majority carrier sets the type, and the Hall effect shows its sign.
m band structure with impurity bands near the conduction band (donors) or the valence band (acceptors); carrier densities of electrons and holes
g hole; impurity atom; doping; donor impurity; n-type semiconductor; impurity band; acceptor impurity; p-type semiconductor; majority carrier; minority carrier
R[thm] doping <- bands, econf : group-V atoms donate, group-III atoms accept an electron | Impurity levels | Valence counting.
P The sign of the carriers is found with the Hall effect. A current I flows along a strip of width w and thickness t in a perpendicular magnetic field B. The carriers move with [[vd|drift velocity]] v_d, the magnetic force pushes them toward one edge, and charge builds up there until its electric force balances the magnetic one. The [[VH|Hall voltage]] across the strip is then V_H = v_{d}Bw. Positive and negative carriers carrying the same current move in opposite directions and are pushed toward the same edge, so V_H has opposite signs for the two. Measurements on a [[doping|doped semiconductor]] give negative carriers in n-type samples and positive carriers in p-type samples, so holes act as positive charges. The more numerous carriers are the *majority carriers*, the others the *minority carriers*. The Hall voltage also gives the majority-carrier density, n = IB/(|q|V_{H}t).
R[meas] doping <- VH : sign of the Hall voltage gives the carrier sign | Hall effect | Holes act as positive carriers.
X drift velocity :: = vd

§ 3 9.7
P Where p-type and n-type material meet, at a *p–n junction*, electrons diffuse into the p side and holes into the n side, and they recombine. This leaves a *depletion layer* without mobile carriers, holding the fixed charges of ionized donors on the n side and ionized acceptors on the p side. Their field drives a drift current that cancels the diffusion current at equilibrium. An applied [[dV|voltage]] V_b shifts the barrier. In the *forward bias configuration*, p side positive, the barrier falls by eV_b, and by the Boltzmann factor, with k_{B} the [[kB|Boltzmann constant]], the number of majority carriers able to cross grows as exp(eV_{b}/k_{B}T). In the *reverse bias configuration* the barrier rises and only a small minority-carrier current I₀ flows. The [[pn|p–n junction]] of two [[doping|doped semiconductors]] thus rectifies. At 300 K, k_{B}T/e ≈ 26 mV, so the forward current grows tenfold every 60 mV. Beyond the *breakdown voltage*, a large reverse bias, the current rises abruptly.
Q I = I₀[exp(eV_{b}/k_{B}T) − 1]
N S pn | p–n junction | I = I₀(e^{eV/k_BT} − 1) | q
d Joining p- and n-type material creates a depletion layer with a built-in field; current flows easily in forward bias and hardly at all in reverse bias, until breakdown.
m depletion layer with built-in potential; current–voltage relation I = I₀(e^{eV_b/k_BT} − 1)
g p-n junction; depletion layer; reverse bias configuration; forward bias configuration; breakdown voltage
R[thm] pn <- doping, kB, dV : diffusion and drift across the depletion layer | Diode equation | Boltzmann factor for carriers crossing the barrier.
P A *junction transistor* combines two junctions back to back. In an npn transistor a thin p-type base separates an n-type emitter from an n-type collector. The emitter–base [[pn|p–n junction]] is forward biased and injects electrons into the base, while the base–collector junction is reverse biased. The base is so thin that most injected electrons cross it before they recombine, and the field of the reverse-biased junction sweeps them into the collector. Only a small *base current* I_B flows, while the *collector current* I_C is far larger: I_C = βI_B, with the current gain β typically about 100. A small signal at the base thus controls a large current, and the [[transistor|junction transistor]] works as an *amplifier*. Driven fully on or fully off, it works as a switch, the basic element of digital circuits.
N M transistor | Junction transistor | I_C = βI_B | q
d Two junctions back to back: a small base current controls a large collector current, so the device amplifies.
p current gain β; amplifier and switch; built from two p–n junctions
g junction transistor; base current; collector current; amplifier
R[thm] transistor <- pn : thin base between two junctions | Two junctions | Carriers injected into the base reach the collector.

§ 3 9.8
P Below its *critical temperature* T_c, a material in the [[supercond|superconducting state]] has zero resistance and expels magnetic field from its interior. A strong enough [[Bval|magnetic field]] destroys the state. The [[Bc|critical magnetic field]] B_c, measured by raising the applied field at fixed [[temp|temperature]] until resistance returns, closely follows the parabola below and falls to zero at T_c. For lead, T_c = 7.2 K and B_c(0) = 0.080 T, so at 3.6 K, where T/T_c = ½, B_c = 0.75 × 0.080 T = 0.060 T. A current I produces the [[Bwire|field of a long straight wire]], μ₀I/(2πR) at the surface of a wire of radius R, so B_c also limits the current a superconducting wire can carry. A *type I superconductor*, such as lead, loses superconductivity abruptly at B_c. A *type II superconductor* passes through a mixed state in which the field enters along thin tubes; niobium–titanium alloy stays superconducting up to about 10 T at 4 K and is used in MRI magnets.
Q B_c(T) = B_c(0)[1 − (T/T_c)²]
N O Bc | Critical magnetic field | B_c(T) = B_c(0)[1 − (T/T_c)²] | q
d Field above which superconductivity is destroyed; type I superconductors switch abruptly, type II pass through a mixed state.
g critical temperature; critical magnetic field; type I superconductor; type II superconductor
R[meas] Bc <- supercond, Bval, temp : B_c(T) = B_c(0)[1 − (T/T_c)²] | Measured phase boundary | Zero at T_c.

P The explanation came in 1957 from John Bardeen, Leon Cooper and Robert Schrieffer and is called [[bcs|BCS theory]]. An electron moving through the [[lattice|lattice]] pulls nearby positive ions slightly toward its path, leaving a region of excess positive charge that attracts a second electron. Through this effective attraction, two electrons of the [[fermigas|free electron gas]] with energies near E_F, opposite momenta and opposite spins form a *Cooper pair*. Pairs have integer spin, are not limited by the exclusion principle, and condense into a single quantum state. Breaking a pair costs an energy gap of order k_{B}T_c. Below T_c, scattering that would change the motion of one electron must break a pair, and too little thermal energy is available, so the condensate flows without loss: [[supercond|superconductivity]] follows. Heavier isotopes of a metal have lower T_c, as expected if lattice vibrations cause the pairing. Copper oxides that superconduct above 77 K are not explained by this simple form of the theory.
N M bcs | BCS theory | Cooper pairs condense | q
d Lattice vibrations bind electrons into Cooper pairs, which condense into one quantum state that carries current without resistance.
p pairing through the lattice; energy gap; macroscopic quantum state; explains T_c and zero resistance
g BCS theory; Cooper pair
R[thm] bcs <- fermigas, lattice : electron–lattice attraction binds pairs near E_F | Pairing | An effective attraction through the lattice.
R[thm] supercond <- bcs : condensed pairs scatter without energy loss | Superconductivity explained | The phenomenon of Volume 2, Chapter 9 explained.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 10
§ 3 10.0
P The [[nucatom|nuclear model of the atom]] placed almost all of an atom's mass in a nucleus only a few femtometres (10⁻¹⁵ m) across, tens of thousands of times smaller than the atom. This chapter turns to the nucleus itself. It is made of protons and neutrons, held together by a force much stronger than the Coulomb repulsion between the protons. Nuclear energies are millions of electronvolts, large enough that the change in [[Erel|rest energy]], Δmc², is a measurable fraction of the mass. The same ideas explain radioactive decay, the energy of fission and fusion, the dating of old materials, and the medical uses and hazards of radiation.
§ 3 10.1
P The [[nucatom|nuclear model of the atom]] left open what the nucleus is made of. The *atomic nucleus* contains Z *protons*, each of charge +e, and N *neutrons*, which are uncharged and slightly heavier; together they are *nucleons*. The *atomic number* Z fixes the nuclear charge, hence the number of electrons in the neutral atom and its chemistry; electrons and protons are the [[atom|charge carriers]] of ordinary matter. The *neutron number* N adds mass without charge, and the *mass number* A = Z + N counts the nucleons. A [[nuclide|nuclide]] is a nucleus with given Z and N, written ᴬ_{Z}X; ²³⁸₉₂U, for example, has 146 neutrons. *Isotopes* have the same Z but different N, as do ordinary hydrogen ¹H, *deuterium* ²H and *tritium* ³H.
N S nuclide | Nuclide | ᴬ_Z X | q
d A nucleus specified by its atomic number Z (protons) and neutron number N; mass number A = Z + N counts nucleons. Isotopes share Z. Radius r = r₀A^{1/3}; masses in atomic mass units.
m pair (Z, N) of nucleon numbers; chart of the nuclides plots N against Z
g atomic nucleus; nucleons; atomic number; neutron number; mass number; nuclide; chart of the nuclides; isotopes; atomic mass; atomic mass unit; radius of a nucleus
R[def] nuclide <- nucatom, atom : nucleus of Z protons and N neutrons | Composition of the nucleus | Neutrons add mass without charge.
P Two protons 1 fm apart repel each other, by [[coul|Coulomb's law]], with a force of about 230 N. Nuclei hold together because of the [[strong|strong nuclear force]], an attraction between any two nucleons that is much stronger than the Coulomb force at about 1 fm but falls to almost nothing beyond a few femtometres. It acts equally between protons and neutrons, and it saturates: each nucleon attracts only its nearest neighbours, while the Coulomb repulsion acts between every pair of protons. Light stable nuclei therefore have N ≈ Z, but heavy ones need extra neutrons, which add attraction without repulsion; lead-208 has Z = 82 and N = 126. On the *chart of the nuclides*, which plots N against Z, the stable [[nuclide|nuclides]] form a narrow band that bends away from the line N = Z toward more neutrons.
N M strong | Strong nuclear force | short-range attraction | q ax
d Attraction between nucleons, much stronger than the Coulomb repulsion at about 1 fm but negligible beyond a few fm.
p short range (~fm); charge independent; saturates (each nucleon binds only neighbours)
g strong nuclear force
R[thm] nuclide <- strong, coul : stable nuclei balance strong attraction and Coulomb repulsion; N > Z for heavy nuclei | Stability | Extra neutrons add attraction without repulsion.
P Nuclear sizes are measured by scattering high-energy electrons from nuclei. The *radius of a nucleus* follows r = r₀A^{1/3} with r₀ ≈ 1.2 fm, so ²³⁸U has r ≈ 1.2 fm × 6.20 = 7.4 fm. The volume is proportional to A, so all nuclei have about the same density, 2.3 × 10¹⁷ kg/m³. Masses are given in *atomic mass units*: 1 u = 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, one-twelfth of the mass of a ¹²C atom. The proton has 1.007 276 u and the neutron 1.008 665 u. The *atomic mass* of an element is the average over its isotopes, weighted by abundance. Chlorine, 75.8 % ³⁵Cl at 34.969 u and 24.2 % ³⁷Cl at 36.966 u, has atomic mass 0.758 × 34.969 u + 0.242 × 36.966 u = 35.45 u.

§ 3 10.2
P A nucleus has less mass than its separated nucleons. The *mass defect* Δm = Zm_p + Nm_n − m_{nuc} measures the difference, and by the equivalence of mass and [[Erel|rest energy]] the [[BE|binding energy]] E_b = Δmc² is the energy needed to pull the [[nuclide|nuclide]] apart into free nucleons. With atomic masses, Zm(¹H) replaces Zm_p so that the electron masses cancel. For helium-4, Δm = 2(1.007 825 u) + 2(1.008 665 u) − 4.002 603 u = 0.030 377 u. Since 1 u corresponds to 931.5 MeV, E_b = 28.3 MeV, or 7.07 MeV per nucleon. The *binding energy per nucleon* (BEN), E_b/A, rises steeply among light nuclei, peaks at about 8.8 MeV near iron and nickel (A ≈ 56–62), and falls slowly to 7.6 MeV for uranium. The 7 MeV that binds a nucleon in helium is about 5 × 10⁵ times the 13.6 eV that binds the electron in hydrogen.
Q E_b = (Zm_p + Nm_n − m_{nuc})c²
N O BE | Binding energy | E_b = Δm c² | q
d Energy needed to separate a nucleus into its nucleons, equal to the mass defect times c²; binding energy per nucleon peaks near iron.
g mass defect; binding energy (BE); binding energy per nucleon (BEN)
R[thm] BE <- nuclide, Erel : Δm = Zm_p + Nm_n − m_nuc;  E_b = Δm c² | Mass defect | Rest energy of the parts minus that of the whole.

§ 3 10.3
P Some nuclei are unstable and decay spontaneously. This *radioactivity* was discovered by Henri Becquerel in 1896, in radiation from uranium salts. When a given nucleus will *decay* cannot be predicted; quantum mechanics gives only a probability, in the sense of the [[born|Born rule]]. Each [[nuclide|nucleus]] of a given kind has the same probability λ dt of decaying in the next interval dt, whatever its age, where λ is the *decay constant*. Of N nuclei, λN dt decay, so −dN/dt = λN, and integration gives the [[decaylaw|radioactive decay law]]. The *half-life* T½ = ln 2/λ = 0.693/λ is the time in which half of any sample decays, and the mean *lifetime* is 1/λ. The *activity* A = λN, the number of decays per second, falls in the same way. Its SI unit is the *becquerel*, 1 Bq = 1 decay/s; the older *curie* is 1 Ci = 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq.
Q N = N₀e^{−λt},   T½ = 0.693/λ
N M decaylaw | Radioactive decay law | N = N₀ e^{−λt} | q
d Unstable nuclei decay at random with a constant probability λ per unit time, so their number and activity fall exponentially: half-life T½ = 0.693/λ, mean lifetime 1/λ.
p first-order: dN/dt = −λN; memoryless; activity A = λN in becquerel (1 Ci = 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq)
g radioactivity; decay; decay constant; radioactive decay law; half-life; lifetime; activity; becquerel (Bq); curie (Ci)
R[law] decaylaw <- nuclide, born : −dN/dt = λN | Constant decay probability | Each nucleus decays independently with probability λ dt.
P Because decay proceeds at a known rate, the fraction of a radioactive isotope that remains measures time. Inverting the [[decaylaw|decay law]] gives t = (1/λ) ln(N₀/N), the basis of [[dating|radioactive dating]]; it needs the initial amount N₀. In *carbon-14 dating*, cosmic rays keep making ¹⁴C in the atmosphere, so living organisms, which exchange carbon with the air, hold a nearly constant ratio of ¹⁴C to ¹²C. After death the exchange stops and the ¹⁴C decays with a half-life of 5730 years. A sample whose ¹⁴C activity per gram of carbon is 60 % of that in living matter is 5730 y × log₂(1/0.60) ≈ 4.22 × 10³ years old. The method works up to several half-lives, roughly 50 000 years. Rocks are dated with longer-lived isotopes such as uranium-238, whose half-life is 4.5 × 10⁹ years.
N M dating | Radioactive dating | t = ln(N₀/N)/λ | q
d Ages from the fraction of a radioactive isotope left, as in carbon-14 dating of once-living material.
p needs known initial ratio; ¹⁴C half-life 5730 y; range limited to a few half-lives
g radioactive dating; carbon-14 dating
R[thm] dating <- decaylaw : t = (1/λ) ln(N₀/N) | Invert the decay law | Measured activity gives N.

§ 3 10.4
P Nuclear radiation comes in three kinds, first told apart by how far they penetrate matter and how a magnetic field bends them. *Alpha (α) rays* are helium nuclei, ⁴He, and are stopped by a sheet of paper. *Beta (β) rays* are electrons or, in some decays, *positrons*, the *antielectrons* that have the electron's mass and the opposite charge; they penetrate a few millimetres of aluminium. *Gamma (γ) rays* are high-energy photons, and stopping them takes centimetres of lead. The electrons of β rays are not present in the nucleus beforehand. By the [[uncert|Heisenberg uncertainty principle]], an electron confined within 10 fm would have pc of order 10 MeV or more (Δp ≥ ħ/2Δx), well above the energies of β electrons, so they are created in the decay.
P In the [[decay|nuclear decays]] a *parent nucleus* turns into a *daughter nucleus*. *Alpha decay* lowers Z by 2 and A by 4, as in ²³⁸U → ²³⁴Th + ⁴He. In *beta decay* a neutron in the nucleus becomes a proton and emits an electron and an *antineutrino*, as in ¹⁴C → ¹⁴N + e⁻ + ν̄; in β⁺ decay a proton becomes a neutron and emits a positron and a *neutrino*. In *gamma decay* a nucleus in an excited state emits a photon, and Z and A are unchanged. Every decay conserves [[qcons|charge]] and nucleon number. The energy released is Q = (m_{parent} − m_{products})c²; for α decay it equals the [[BE|binding energy]] of the products minus that of the parent [[nuclide|nuclide]]. For the α decay of ²³⁸U, the atomic masses give Q = 4.27 MeV.
Q ᴬ_{Z}X → ᴬ⁻⁴_{Z−2}Y + ⁴₂He,   ᴬ_{Z}X → ᴬ_{Z+1}Y + e⁻ + ν̄
N S decay | Nuclear decays | α, β⁻, β⁺, γ | q
d A parent nucleus becomes a daughter: α decay emits a helium nucleus, β⁻ decay an electron and an antineutrino, β⁺ decay a positron (antielectron) and a neutrino, γ decay a photon from an excited nucleus. Heavy nuclei decay in series; transuranic elements are made artificially.
m transitions (Z, N) → (Z − 2, N − 2) + α; (Z + 1, N − 1) + e⁻ + ν̄; (Z − 1, N + 1) + e⁺ + ν; excited → ground + γ
g alpha decay; alpha (α) rays; beta decay; beta (β) rays; gamma decay; gamma (γ) rays; parent nucleus; daughter nucleus; positron; antielectrons; neutrino; antineutrino; decay series; transuranic element
R[thm] decay <- nuclide, BE, qcons : charge and nucleon number conserved; released energy from mass difference | Decay equations | Bookkeeping of Z, A and energy.
P Alpha half-lives range from less than a microsecond to more than 10¹⁰ years, while α energies span only a few MeV. Classically an α particle could not escape at all. Inside the nucleus the strong force holds it in a well; outside, the Coulomb repulsion of the daughter forms a barrier about 30 MeV high for uranium, far above the 4.27 MeV available. Quantum mechanically the α particle tunnels through the barrier, with a [[Ttun|tunneling probability]] that falls exponentially with the barrier's width and with the square root of its height above the particle's energy. A higher energy meets a thinner and lower barrier, so the [[decay|decay]] is far faster. Thus ²³⁸U, with Q = 4.27 MeV, has a half-life of 4.5 × 10⁹ years, while ²¹²Po, with Q = 8.95 MeV, has a half-life of 0.30 μs, more than 10²³ times shorter.
R[thm] decay <- Ttun : α particle tunnels through the Coulomb barrier | Alpha decay as tunneling | Explains the huge range of α half-lives.
P A daughter nucleus may itself be unstable, so heavy nuclei decay through a *decay series*. Uranium-238 reaches stable lead-206 after eight α decays and six β⁻ decays: A falls by 8 × 4 = 32, and Z falls by 16 and rises by 6, from 92 to 82. Elements beyond uranium, the *transuranic elements*, occur in nature only in traces and are made artificially. Plutonium-239, for example, forms when ²³⁸U captures a neutron and the resulting ²³⁹U undergoes two β⁻ decays, through neptunium-239.

§ 3 10.5
P In the 1930s several groups, starting with Enrico Fermi's, bombarded uranium with neutrons. In 1938 Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann found barium, a medium-mass element, among the products: the uranium nucleus had split. In [[fission|fission]] a heavy nucleus such as ²³⁵U absorbs a slow neutron and divides into two fragments of unequal mass plus two or three neutrons, for example n + ²³⁵U → ¹⁴¹Ba + ⁹²Kr + 3n. About 200 MeV is released, mostly as kinetic energy of the fragments. The new neutrons can cause further fissions, a *chain reaction*. If on average each fission leads to exactly one more, the multiplication factor is k = 1 and the system is in a state of *criticality*. Neutrons escape through the surface, whose area grows more slowly than the volume, so k = 1 is reached only above a *critical mass* of fissile material.
N S fission | Fission | heavy nucleus → two fragments + neutrons | q
d A heavy nucleus such as ²³⁵U splits after absorbing a neutron, releasing about 200 MeV and further neutrons; a chain reaction is critical when each fission causes one more on average (critical mass).
m reaction n + ²³⁵U → fragments + 2–3 n + energy; chain reaction with multiplication factor k (k = 1 critical)
g fission; criticality; critical mass
P Niels Bohr and John Wheeler explained fission in 1939 with the [[liqdrop|liquid drop model]]. Nuclear density is nearly constant, like that of a liquid, because the [[strong|strong force]] is short-range and saturates, as do the forces between molecules in a [[fluid|fluid]]. Nucleons at the surface have fewer neighbours, which acts like a surface tension and favours a spherical shape, while the [[coul|Coulomb repulsion]] of the protons acts across the whole drop and favours breaking it. When ²³⁵U absorbs a neutron, the excited drop oscillates; if it stretches far enough, the Coulomb repulsion between its two ends wins and it splits. The energy release follows from the [[BE|binding energy]] per nucleon: about 7.6 MeV for uranium against about 8.5 MeV for fragments of medium mass. A gain of about 0.9 MeV for each of 236 nucleons gives roughly 200 MeV per [[fission|fission]].
N M liqdrop | Liquid drop model | nucleus as a charged drop | q
d Treats the nucleus as a drop of incompressible nuclear fluid held by surface tension (strong force) and pushed apart by Coulomb repulsion; deformation splits a heavy drop.
p binding from volume, surface and Coulomb terms; explains fission of heavy nuclei
g liquid drop model
R[analog] liqdrop <- strong, coul, fluid : nuclear density constant, like a liquid | Drop analogy | Short-range saturating force behaves like surface tension.
R[thm] fission <- liqdrop, BE : heavy nuclei have lower binding energy per nucleon than the fragments | Energy release | Moving toward the peak of the BEN curve.
X breeder reactor :: a reactor design (application)
P A *nuclear reactor* holds the chain reaction at k = 1. Fission of ²³⁵U is most likely with slow neutrons, so a moderator such as water or graphite slows the neutrons, and control rods that absorb neutrons adjust k. Natural uranium is only 0.72 % ²³⁵U, and most reactors use fuel enriched in it. The first reactor, built under Enrico Fermi's direction in Chicago, went critical in 1942. An *atomic bomb* instead assembles a mass with k > 1 so quickly that many generations of fission occur within about a microsecond, before the material blows apart. A *breeder reactor* uses neutrons to turn ²³⁸U into fissile plutonium-239 and so produces more fuel than it consumes.

§ 3 10.6
P Light nuclei lie on the rising side of the curve of [[BE|binding energy]] per nucleon, so joining them releases energy. In [[fusion|nuclear fusion]] such as ²H + ³H → ⁴He + n, the masses before and after give a released energy, the *Q value*, of 17.6 MeV. Fusion is hard to start because the nuclei must come within a few femtometres against their Coulomb repulsion, a barrier of several hundred keV for two protons. In the Sun's core, at about 1.5 × 10⁷ K, the mean thermal energy is only about 2 keV. Fusion still occurs for two reasons. The high-energy tail of the [[mbd|Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution]] contains a small fraction of much faster protons, and these pass the rest of the barrier by [[Ttun|tunneling]]. The product of the falling tail and the rising tunneling probability sets the rate.
N S fusion | Fusion | light nuclei → heavier nucleus | q
d Light nuclei combine and release energy; in the Sun four protons become helium by the proton–proton chain. Stars build heavier elements (nucleosynthesis).
m reactions such as ¹H + ¹H → ²H + e⁺ + ν; proton–proton chain 4¹H → ⁴He + 2e⁺ + 2ν + energy
g nuclear fusion; proton-proton chain; nucleosynthesis
R[thm] fusion <- BE, Ttun, mbd : thermal protons tunnel through the Coulomb barrier | Fusion in stars | Only the fastest protons, helped by tunneling, fuse.
X nuclear fusion reactor :: a reactor design (application)
P In the late 1930s Hans Bethe worked out how stars release energy by fusion. In the Sun the main route is the *proton–proton chain*. Two protons fuse to deuterium, ¹H + ¹H → ²H + e⁺ + ν, a slow step because it needs the weak interaction that also causes β decay. Then ²H + ¹H → ³He + γ, and two ³He nuclei form ⁴He and two protons. The net result, 4¹H → ⁴He + 2e⁺ + 2ν, releases 26.7 MeV, part of it carried off by the neutrinos. Older stars fuse helium into carbon and continue toward iron, beyond which fusion no longer releases energy; heavier elements form by neutron capture. This *nucleosynthesis* made the carbon, oxygen, iron and other heavy elements around us. A *nuclear fusion reactor* would fuse deuterium and tritium in a plasma above 10⁸ K, confined by magnetic fields or compressed by lasers; such reactors remain under development.

§ 3 10.7
P Radioactive isotopes are used in medicine because their radiation can be detected from outside the body. A *radiopharmaceutical* is a compound that an organ takes up and that carries a *radioactive tag*. Iodine-131 collects in the thyroid, and technetium-99m, which emits a 140 keV γ ray with a half-life of 6.0 h, is the most widely used tag. In *single-photon-emission computed tomography* (SPECT), detectors rotating around the patient record the γ rays from many directions, and a computer reconstructs a three-dimensional map of the tracer. *Positron emission tomography* (PET) uses positron emitters such as fluorine-18. Each positron annihilates with an electron into two 511 keV photons travelling in opposite directions, and detecting both at once fixes a line through the point of emission.
P The same radiation damages tissue. Each α particle, β particle or γ photon from [[decay|nuclear decays]] carries an energy of order 1 MeV, while ionizing a molecule takes about 10 eV, so one particle can ionize tens of thousands of molecules and break bonds in DNA. The effect grows with the energy absorbed per unit [[m|mass]], the [[dose|radiation dose]]. Its SI unit is the gray, 1 Gy = 1 J/kg; the older *radiation dose unit*, the *rad*, is 0.01 J/kg. Equal doses of different radiations do unequal harm, so the dose is multiplied by the *relative biological effectiveness* (RBE), about 1 for X-rays, γ rays and electrons and up to about 20 for α particles. The product is the equivalent dose, in *sieverts* (Sv) for a dose in grays and in *roentgen equivalent man* (rem) for a dose in rad; 1 Sv = 100 rem.
N O dose | Radiation dose | rad, rem, Sv |
d Energy absorbed per kilogram of tissue (rad), weighted by the relative biological effectiveness of the radiation to give the equivalent dose (rem, sievert).
g radiation dose unit (rad); relative biological effectiveness (RBE); roentgen equivalent man (rem); sievert (Sv)
R[def] dose <- decay, m : dose = absorbed energy / mass;  equivalent dose = RBE × dose | Energy per mass | The biological weight depends on the kind of radiation.
X high dose; low dose; moderate dose; positron emission tomography (PET); radioactive tags; radiopharmaceutical; single-photon-emission computed tomography (SPECT) :: medical applications and dose ranges
P Effects depend on the dose and on how quickly it is received. *Low doses*, below about 0.1 Sv, cause no immediate symptoms; their main risk is a small increase in the chance of cancer years later. *Moderate doses*, from about 0.1 to 1 Sv, can cause temporary changes in blood counts. *High doses* of more than about 1 Sv received at once cause radiation sickness, and about 4–5 Sv without treatment is fatal to roughly half of those exposed. Natural sources, mainly radon gas, cosmic rays and radioactive potassium in the body, give a typical person about 2–3 mSv per year. As an example, 2.0 mJ of γ-ray energy absorbed by 1.0 kg of tissue is a dose of 2.0 mGy and an equivalent dose of 2.0 mSv; the same energy deposited by α particles would give about 40 mSv.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 11
§ 3 11.0
P Atoms are built from electrons and nuclei, and nuclei from protons and neutrons held by the [[strong|strong nuclear force]]. Particle physics asks what these are made of and which interactions act between the smallest constituents. The answer so far is a short list of elementary particles, a set of conservation laws that decide which reactions occur, and one theory for three of the four fundamental interactions. The chapter then turns to the largest scale. Galaxies recede from one another, and the universe has cooled from a hot, dense early state in which particle physics decided what formed. [[GR|General relativity]] describes the expansion.
§ 3 11.1
P All known forces reduce to four [[forces4|fundamental forces]]. The [[strong|strong nuclear force]] binds nucleons and, at a deeper level, the quarks inside them; its carriers are *gluons*. The electromagnetic force, described for static charges by [[coul|Coulomb's law]], is carried by photons. Gravity, described by [[ngrav|Newton's law of gravitation]], acts on all mass and energy; its proposed carrier, the graviton, has not been observed. A fourth force, the *weak nuclear force*, is needed for β [[decay|decay]], in which a neutron turns into a proton; its carriers are the massive *W and Z bosons*. The strengths differ enormously: between two protons, gravity is weaker than the electric force by the factor Gm_{p}²/(ke²) ≈ 8 × 10⁻³⁷. At energies near 100 GeV the electromagnetic and weak forces merge into one *electroweak force*. A *grand unified theory* would join the strong force to it at much higher energies, and a *theory of everything* would include gravity; neither has been confirmed.
N M forces4 | Fundamental forces | strong, electromagnetic, weak, gravitational | q
d Four fundamental forces, each carried by bosons: gluons (strong), photons (electromagnetic), W and Z bosons (weak), gravitons (gravity, unobserved). The electromagnetic and weak forces unify into the electroweak force; grand unified theories and a theory of everything are sought.
p very different strengths and ranges; electroweak unification at high energy; gravity not yet unified
g fundamental force; weak nuclear force; gluon; W and Z boson; electroweak force; grand unified theory; theory of everything
R[gen] forces4 <- strong, coul, ngrav, decay : nuclear, electric, gravitational forces and β decay | Four interactions | Beta decay needs a fourth, weak, force.
P These forces act on a small set of particles. The electrons and protons that are the [[atom|charge carriers]] of matter, the neutron, the neutrinos and positrons of β [[decay|decay]], and many short-lived particles found since are sorted first by [[spin|spin]]. *Fermions* have half-integer spin and *bosons* integer spin, and their *exchange symmetry* differs. The state of two identical fermions changes sign when they are exchanged, so fermions obey the [[pauli|exclusion principle]]; that of two identical bosons does not, and any number of bosons can share one state. Among the [[particles|elementary particles]], the fermions form two families. *Leptons*, the electron, muon and tau and a neutrino for each, feel no strong force. *Quarks* feel it and occur only inside composite particles, the *hadrons*: *baryons*, such as the proton and neutron, contain three quarks, and *mesons*, such as the pion, a quark and an antiquark. Every particle has an *antiparticle* with the same mass and opposite charge; the *positron* is the electron's.
N S particles | Elementary particles | leptons, quarks, bosons | q
d Matter is made of fermions (half-integer spin): leptons such as the electron and neutrino, and quarks, which form hadrons (baryons from three quarks, mesons from a quark and an antiquark). Every particle has an antiparticle, such as the positron. Bosons carry the forces.
m classification by spin (fermion/boson), charges and quantum numbers; antiparticles with opposite charges; exchange symmetry antisymmetric for fermions
g fermion; boson; lepton; hadron; baryons; mesons; quark; antiparticle; positron; exchange symmetry
R[gen] particles <- atom, decay, spin, pauli : electrons, nucleons, neutrinos and their antiparticles classified | From atoms to particles | Spin and the exclusion principle sort fermions from bosons.
X strong nuclear force :: = strong

§ 3 11.2
P Some reactions that conserve energy, momentum and [[qcons|charge]] are never observed. The decay p → e⁺ + γ, for example, has never been seen, and the proton's lifetime exceeds 10³³ years. Such absences are explained by further additive quantum numbers carried by [[particles|particles]]; these are the [[pcons2|particle conservation laws]]. *Baryon number* B is +1 for baryons, −1 for antibaryons and 0 for leptons and mesons. *Lepton number* is +1 for leptons, −1 for antileptons and 0 otherwise. It is counted separately for the electron, muon and tau families, and only neutrino oscillations, in which a neutrino changes family, alter these family numbers. In neutron decay, n → p + e⁻ + ν̄ₑ, B is 1 before and after, and the electron's lepton number +1 is cancelled by the antineutrino's −1. *Strangeness* S is conserved by the strong and electromagnetic interactions but not by the weak one. The Λ⁰, with S = −1, therefore decays to p + π⁻ only weakly and slowly, with a lifetime of 2.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ s.
N M pcons2 | Particle conservation laws | baryon number, lepton number, strangeness | q
d Reactions conserve charge, baryon number and lepton number; strangeness is conserved by the strong and electromagnetic forces but not the weak force.
p additive quantum numbers; conserved in every observed reaction (baryon number, total lepton number; the separate electron, muon and tau numbers are broken by neutrino oscillations) or only by strong and electromagnetic interactions (strangeness); forbid reactions such as p → e⁺ + γ
g baryon number; lepton number; strangeness
R[gen] pcons2 <- qcons, particles : additive quantum numbers conserved like charge | More conserved charges | Read off from which reactions never occur.

§ 3 11.3
P In 1964 Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig independently proposed that hadrons are made of quarks, constituents listed among the [[particles|elementary particles]]. Six are now known: up, charm and top, with charge +⅔e, and down, strange and bottom, with charge −⅓e, each with baryon number ⅓. In the [[quarkmodel|quark model]] the proton is uud, with charge (⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓)e = +e; the neutron is udd, with charge 0; and the π⁺ meson is ud̄, with charge (⅔ + ⅓)e = +e. The [[pcons2|conserved quantities]] of a hadron are sums over its quarks; a strange quark has S = −1. The Ω⁻ baryon, sss with spin 3/2, has three identical quarks in the same state, which the [[pauli|exclusion principle]] forbids. A new quantum number, *colour*, with three values, removes the conflict: the three quarks differ in colour. Only colourless combinations occur, and no free quark has been seen. Electron scattering at high energy reveals point-like charges inside the proton, the main evidence for quarks.
N M quarkmodel | Quark model | baryon = qqq,  meson = qq̄ | q
d Hadrons are built from quarks with fractional charges; a new quantum number, color, lets three identical quarks share a state, and only colourless combinations exist.
p fractional charges ±⅓e, ±⅔e; three colors; quarks confined in hadrons
g color
R[thm] quarkmodel <- particles, pauli, pcons2 : quantum numbers of hadrons from constituent quarks | Constituents | Color needed so that the exclusion principle holds.

§ 3 11.4
P Particles are studied by accelerating them and recording what their collisions produce. A *particle accelerator* gives charged particles energy with electric fields: a *Van de Graaff accelerator* uses one large static [[dV|voltage]], a *linear accelerator* a long row of oscillating fields. A *cyclotron* bends the particles into [[helix|circular orbits]] in a fixed magnetic field so that they cross the same gap many times. A *synchrotron* raises the field as the [[prel|momentum]] grows, keeping the radius fixed; its particles lose energy as *synchrotron radiation*, far more for electrons than for protons of equal energy. Colliding beams head-on makes their whole energy available: the *Large Hadron Collider*, a 27 km ring, collides protons at 13.6 TeV. In a *particle detector*, a track of radius r in a field B gives p = qBr, or p ≈ 0.3Br in GeV/c for a singly charged particle, with B in teslas and r in metres.
X particle accelerator; particle detector; synchrotron; synchrotron radiation :: instruments of particle physics

§ 3 11.5
P The [[sm|Standard Model]] describes three of the [[forces4|fundamental forces]] as the exchange of bosons. *Quantum electrodynamics* (QED) treats the electromagnetic force as the exchange of [[photon|photons]] between charges; *quantum chromodynamics* (QCD) treats the strong force as the exchange of gluons between the colour charges of [[quarkmodel|quarks]]; the electroweak theory adds the W and Z bosons. A *Feynman diagram*, named after Richard Feynman, draws such a process with time running upward, lines for particles and vertices where a boson is emitted or absorbed. The exchanged boson is a *virtual particle*. Creating it violates energy conservation by ΔE = mc², which the [[uncertE|energy–time uncertainty]] relation tolerates for a time Δt ≈ ħ/(mc²). Moving at most at c, the boson travels R ≈ cΔt = ħ/(mc), which sets the range of the force. For the W boson, mc² = 80.4 GeV and R ≈ 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁸ m; the massless photon gives the electromagnetic force an infinite range.
Q R ≈ ħc/(mc²)
N M sm | Standard Model | QED + electroweak + QCD | q
d The theory of the strong, weak and electromagnetic forces: forces arise from the exchange of virtual particles, pictured in Feynman diagrams; quantum electrodynamics (QED) and quantum chromodynamics (QCD) are its parts.
p gauge theory; virtual bosons exchanged within ΔE Δt ≥ ħ/2; range ~ ħ/(mc) for a carrier of mass m; gravity not included
g Standard Model; quantum electrodynamics (QED); quantum chromodynamics (QCD); Feynman diagram; virtual particle
R[thm] sm <- forces4, quarkmodel, uncertE, photon : force range ≈ ħc/(mc²) of the exchanged boson | Exchange of virtual bosons | Energy–time uncertainty allows a short-lived carrier.
P The Higgs boson, found at the Large Hadron Collider in 2012, completes the [[sm|Standard Model]]; its field gives the W and Z bosons, the quarks and the charged leptons their masses. The model does not include gravity, and it does not account for dark matter. The strengths of the strong, weak and electromagnetic forces change with the energy of a collision and approach one another near 10¹⁵ GeV. This is the main motive for a grand unified theory, in which the three would be one force. Such theories predict that protons decay, which has not been observed.

§ 3 11.6
P Light from distant galaxies arrives with its spectral lines shifted to longer wavelengths, a *redshift* z = (λ_{obs} − λ_{emit})/λ_{emit}. Read as a [[relDop|relativistic Doppler effect]], it gives the recession speed, v ≈ cz when z ≪ 1. Distances come from standard candles, objects of known luminosity such as Cepheid variable stars, whose apparent brightness falls as 1/d² with the distance d, a [[L|length]] of millions of light-years. In 1929 Edwin Hubble found that the speed grows in proportion to the distance. This is [[hubble|Hubble's law]], with *Hubble's constant* H₀ ≈ 70 km/s/Mpc, where 1 Mpc = 3.09 × 10²² m. A galaxy 100 Mpc away therefore recedes at about 7000 km/s, and its redshift is z ≈ 0.023.
Q v = H₀d
N O hubble | Hubble's law | v = H₀ d | rel
d Distant galaxies recede with speeds proportional to their distance, measured from the redshift of their light; H₀ is Hubble's constant.
g Hubble’s law; Hubble’s constant; redshift
R[meas] hubble <- relDop, L : speed from redshift, distance from standard candles | Galaxy redshifts (1929) | Linear relation between v and d.
P Hubble's law has no centre: if every distance grows by the same factor, every galaxy sees all others receding with v ∝ d. Dots on an inflating balloon behave the same way, and no dot on the surface is special. *Cosmology*, the study of the universe as a whole, assumes the *cosmological principle*, that on large scales the universe is the same everywhere and in every direction. In [[GR|general relativity]] the expansion is a stretching of space itself, not a motion of galaxies through space, and the stretching also lengthens light waves, which is the cosmological redshift. Running the expansion of [[hubble|Hubble's law]] backward brings all matter together. The [[bigbang|Big Bang model]] holds that the universe expanded from an extremely hot, dense state. At a constant rate the time since then would be 1/H₀ = 4.4 × 10¹⁷ s ≈ 14 billion years; detailed models give 13.8 billion years.
N M bigbang | Big Bang model | expanding universe | rel
d The universe expanded from an extremely hot, dense early state; cosmology studies this history.
p expansion of space, not motion through it; age ≈ 1/H₀; hot early universe cooled as it expanded
g Big Bang; cosmology
R[thm] bigbang <- hubble, GR : run the expansion backward | Expanding space-time | General relativity describes the expansion.

§ 3 11.7
P The early universe was hot, and as it expanded it cooled, so its history is a sequence of stages set by temperature. At the highest energies the electromagnetic and weak [[forces4|fundamental forces]] acted as one electroweak force, and they separated as the temperature fell. Within about the first ten microseconds, quarks combined into protons and neutrons. During the first few minutes, [[fusion|nucleosynthesis]] built about a quarter of the mass of ordinary matter into helium-4, with traces of deuterium and lithium, the abundances found today in the oldest gas. Until about 380 000 years after the [[bigbang|Big Bang]], at temperatures above about 3000 K, matter was a plasma of nuclei and electrons, opaque to light. Then the electrons joined the nuclei into neutral atoms, and the universe became transparent.
P The radiation released then still fills the universe. Expansion has stretched its wavelengths by a factor of about 1100, so [[bbrad|blackbody radiation]] at 3000 K now has a temperature of 2.7 K and peaks at a wavelength near 1 mm, in the microwave range. Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson detected this [[cmb|cosmic microwave background]] in 1964, reporting it in 1965, as a faint signal arriving equally from all directions. Satellite measurements show that its spectrum is a blackbody to high precision, as the hot early universe of the [[bigbang|Big Bang model]] predicts, and that its temperature varies over the sky by only about one part in 10⁵. Maps of these variations, made by satellites such as the *Wilkinson Microwave Anisotropy Probe* (WMAP), record the small density differences from which galaxies later grew.
N O cmb | Cosmic microwave background | blackbody at 2.7 K | rel
d Radiation left from the hot early universe, now a nearly uniform blackbody spectrum at 2.7 K in the microwave range.
g cosmic microwave background radiation (CMBR)
R[thm] cmb <- bigbang, bbrad : radiation cooled by expansion, still thermal | Relic radiation | Its blackbody spectrum is evidence for a hot early universe.
P Stars far from the centre of a spiral galaxy should orbit more slowly, with the [[vorb|orbital speed]] v = √(GM_{visible}/r) once r encloses most of the visible mass. Measured speeds stay nearly constant out to large r, so the galaxy must contain more mass than it shows. The motions of galaxies in clusters, and the bending of light by clusters predicted by general relativity, called gravitational lensing, lead to the same conclusion. This *dark matter* emits no light and is about five times as abundant as ordinary matter. Observations of distant supernovae, which extend [[hubble|Hubble's law]] to great distances, show that the expansion described by the [[bigbang|Big Bang model]] is speeding up; the cause is called *dark energy*. Together, [[dark|dark matter and dark energy]] make up about 95 % of the energy content of the universe, roughly 27 % and 68 %, and the nature of neither is known.
N O dark | Dark matter and dark energy | Ω_dm, Ω_Λ | rel
d Galaxies rotate too fast for their visible mass (dark matter), and the expansion is accelerating (dark energy); together they make up most of the universe's energy.
g dark matter; dark energy
R[meas] dark <- vorb, hubble, bigbang : rotation speeds above √(GM_visible/r); accelerating expansion | Inferred from motion | Gravity requires more mass than is seen.
X nucleosynthesis :: = fusion
